---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import { PUBLIC_METRICS, metricsCollectedLabel, benchmarksFresh } from '../../config/metrics';

const benchWhen = benchmarksFresh() ? metricsCollectedLabel() : 'January 2026';
const cov = PUBLIC_METRICS.coverage_lines_percent
  ? `${PUBLIC_METRICS.coverage_lines_percent.toFixed(1)}%`
  : 'see release reports';
---

<BaseLayout title="What is PugDB?" description="PugDB is the public name of F4KVS: a Rust-native LSM you can embed. Memoirs and RAG chunks still there after restart.">
  <Header />

  <main class="pt-20 min-h-screen bg-gray-900">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-16">
      <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto prose prose-invert prose-lg">
        <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What is PugDB?</h1>

        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 mb-8">
          <strong>PugDB</strong> is the public name of <strong>F4KVS</strong>: a <strong>Rust-native LSM</strong> you can embed or self-host. Built so a real corpus — memoirs, RAG chunks — is still there after restart, not only faster on a chart.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-green-900/20 rounded-xl p-4 border border-green-700/50 mb-8">
          <p class="text-green-300 text-sm"><strong>Alpha embed</strong> (GO 2026-07-23): C/Go FFI + LSM for memoirs/RAG preprod. Pins {PUBLIC_METRICS.releases?.f4kvs_ffi} + {PUBLIC_METRICS.releases?.f4kvs_lsm}. The server cluster remains <strong>lab</strong> — not a generic production GO. Core KV is well-tested; some distributed features will still change.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="mb-12">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Core Definition</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">PugDB is a high-performance key-value storage system that provides:</p>
          <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Restart proof</strong>: 50× SIGKILL crash-loop with 0 loss; 1.66M soak cache ops with 10/10 anchors; 100k RAG chunks held after reopen</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Low Latency</strong>: P50 latency {PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p50_min}–{PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p50_max}ms, P99 latency {PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p99_min}–{PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p99_max}ms</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Stable Core (Beta)</strong>: {PUBLIC_METRICS.tests_total.toLocaleString('en-US')} automated tests, {PUBLIC_METRICS.ql_tests.toLocaleString('en-US')} QL tests, {cov} QL line coverage (as of {metricsCollectedLabel()})</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Privacy-First Design</strong>: Built with encryption support, audit logging, and offline-first capabilities</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Rust-Native</strong>: Leverages Rust's memory safety and modern async/await patterns</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-12">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Key Characteristics</h2>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">High Performance</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300">The number we care about first is <strong class="text-white">still there after restart</strong>. Product-shaped meso ingest holds 100,050 / 100,050 keys. Server YCSB (10k, partitioned) is {PUBLIC_METRICS.postgres_speedup_avg}× vs PostgreSQL and {PUBLIC_METRICS.scylla_speedup_avg}× vs ScyllaDB ({benchWhen}) — published on /bench, not as a RocksDB pitch. P50 {PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p50_min}–{PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p50_max}ms, P99 {PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p99_min}–{PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p99_max}ms on that campaign.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Rust-Native Architecture</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">Built entirely in Rust, PugDB leverages:</p>
            <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
              <li><strong class="text-white">Async/Await</strong>: Full Tokio integration for excellent concurrency</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Memory Safety</strong>: Rust's compile-time guarantees eliminate entire classes of bugs</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Modern Patterns</strong>: Clean, idiomatic Rust code that's maintainable and performant</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Multi-Language Support via FFI</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">While PugDB is built in Rust, it can be used from any programming language through C-compatible FFI bindings:</p>
            <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
              <li><strong class="text-white">C/C++</strong>: Direct C-compatible API for native integration</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Python, Go, Node.js</strong>: Language bindings can be built on top of the C FFI</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Zero Overhead</strong>: FFI bindings provide direct access to PugDB's core functionality</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Cross-Platform</strong>: Works on all platforms where Rust compiles</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-2">The FFI crate ({PUBLIC_METRICS.releases?.f4kvs_ffi}) is the production embed path — C ABI + Go wrappers over {PUBLIC_METRICS.releases?.f4kvs_lsm}. Other languages can bind the same header.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Stable Core (Beta)</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">The foundation of PugDB is stable and well-tested:</p>
            <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
              <li>Core key-value operations (get, put, delete, exists)</li>
              <li>Memory storage engines (HashMap, BTreeMap) - <strong class="text-white">Beta - Well Tested</strong></li>
              <li>Graceful shutdown and recovery</li>
              <li>Memory leak detection</li>
              <li>Monitoring hooks and metrics collection</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Privacy-Focused Design</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">PugDB is built with privacy and data sovereignty in mind:</p>
            <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
              <li>Encryption support for data at rest and in transit</li>
              <li>Audit logging capabilities</li>
              <li>Offline-first architecture</li>
              <li>Local-first data storage patterns</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Modular Architecture</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">PugDB follows a clean, layered architecture that separates concerns:</p>
            <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
              <li><strong class="text-white">Foundation Layer</strong>: Core abstractions and memory storage</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Extensions Layer</strong>: Advanced storage engines, query language, monitoring</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Distributed Layer</strong>: Server, client, and clustering capabilities</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Cloud Layer</strong>: S3-compatible storage and intelligent tiering</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Application Layer</strong>: CLI tools and utilities</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-12">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Main Features</h2>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Multiple Storage Engines</h3>
            <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
              <li><strong class="text-white">Memory Storage</strong>: HashMap and BTreeMap backends - Beta - Well Tested (813 tests, 80%+ coverage)</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">LSM-Tree Storage</strong>: Beta - Well Tested - 51 unit tests + integration tests, comprehensive crash recovery</li>
              <li><strong class="text-white">Partitioned Storage</strong>: Beta - Well Tested - Extensive integration tests, 6+ test suites, stress-tested concurrency</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Compression Support</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300">Efficient data compression with multiple algorithms: LZ4, Zstd, Gzip, and Snappy.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Monitoring and Metrics</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300">Built-in observability for production deployments: Prometheus metrics integration, health check endpoints, performance monitoring hooks, and resource usage tracking.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Query Language</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300">Beta (Approaching Production) SQL support with {PUBLIC_METRICS.ql_tests.toLocaleString('en-US')}+ tests and {cov} line coverage: Window functions and JOINs GA; subqueries, optimization, and transactions in active hardening.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-12">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Performance Highlights</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">See <a href="/bench/">/bench</a> for product-shaped numbers. The small YCSB A/B/D 10k campaign (last updated: {benchWhen}):</p>
          <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Throughput</strong>: {PUBLIC_METRICS.postgres_speedup_avg}× vs PostgreSQL ({PUBLIC_METRICS.postgres_speedup_min}×–{PUBLIC_METRICS.postgres_speedup_max}×), {PUBLIC_METRICS.scylla_speedup_avg}× vs ScyllaDB ({PUBLIC_METRICS.scylla_speedup_min}×–{PUBLIC_METRICS.scylla_speedup_max}×)</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Latency</strong>: P50 latency {PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p50_min}–{PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p50_max}ms, P99 latency {PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p99_min}–{PUBLIC_METRICS.latency_p99_max}ms</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Batch Operations</strong>: Optimized with connection reuse</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Concurrency</strong>: Excellent scaling up to 32 threads</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Memory Efficiency</strong>: Consistent performance with memory pools</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-4 italic">
            *Benchmark Methodology: Results from single-threaded sequential YCSB workloads. Both PugDB and competitors tested identically. Real-world performance with concurrent workloads may differ.*
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-12">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Quality Metrics</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">PugDB maintains high quality standards with comprehensive testing:</p>
          <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Test Coverage</strong>: {PUBLIC_METRICS.tests_total.toLocaleString('en-US')} automated tests across server monorepo crates (commit {PUBLIC_METRICS.git_commit})</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Code Coverage</strong>: f4kvs-ql {cov} line coverage (smoke tier) — as of {metricsCollectedLabel()}</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Stable Components (Beta)</strong>: Core ({PUBLIC_METRICS.tests_by_package['f4kvs-core']} tests), network ({PUBLIC_METRICS.tests_by_package['f4kvs-network']}), clustering ({PUBLIC_METRICS.clustering_tests})</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Beta Components</strong>: Query language ({PUBLIC_METRICS.ql_tests} tests) and observability ({PUBLIC_METRICS.tests_by_package['f4kvs-observability']} tests)</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-700">
          <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">
            <em>Note: PugDB is the new name for F4KVS. All references to F4KVS in documentation and code will gradually transition to PugDB.</em>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 flex gap-4">
          <a href="/docs" class="text-purple-400 hover:text-purple-300">← Back to Docs</a>
          <a href="/docs/why-pugdb" class="text-purple-400 hover:text-purple-300 ml-auto">Why PugDB? →</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    color: rgb(209 213 219);
  }
  .prose h1, .prose h2, .prose h3 {
    color: rgb(255 255 255);
  }
  .prose a {
    color: rgb(196 181 253);
  }
  .prose a:hover {
    color: rgb(167 139 250);
  }
  .prose strong {
    color: rgb(255 255 255);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>

